+
+## Applied clean from the PR
+
+* `pcsx2/PS2Float.cpp` and `PS2Float.h` — the arithmetic core is byte-for-byte upstream.
+* `pcsx2/FPU.cpp` — every hunk applied essentially verbatim: `checkOverflowUnderflowSoft`,
+  `checkDivideByZeroInvalidSoft`, `fpuCompareFull`, the six `fpuAccurate*` helpers, and the
+  13 op bodies (`ADD_S ADDA_S SUB_S SUBA_S MUL_S MULA_S MADD_S MADDA_S MSUB_S MSUBA_S DIV_S
+  SQRT_S RSQRT_S`) plus the PR's `CVT_S` and `C_cond_S` tweaks.
+* The `Config.h` `SOFT_FLOAT_*` / `CHECK_*` macro block — same names, same semantics.
+
+## Re-derived, and why
+
+### 1. `PS2Float` — three C++17 adaptations (mechanical)
+
+The PR targets a C++20 tree with a `common/BitUtils.h` this fork does not have.
+
+* `std::bit_cast` (C++20) → a `ps2f_bit_cast` memcpy helper in `PS2Float.h`. Two call sites:
+  `PS2Float(float)` and `ToDouble()`. This tree is `cxx_std_17` (`pcsx2/CMakeLists.txt:12`).
+* `common/BitUtils.h` does not exist here. Its three used symbols
+  (`normalizeAmounts`, `BitScanReverse`, `CountLeadingSignBits`) are reproduced in a
+  `PS2FloatBits` namespace at the top of `PS2Float.cpp`; `CountLeadingSignBits` just forwards
+  to this tree's existing `count_leading_sign_bits` in `common/MathUtils.h`.
+* `Console.Error` needs an explicit `#include "../common/Console.h"` here.
+
+**No arithmetic was changed.** Diff `pcsx2/PS2Float.cpp` against
+`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GitHubProUser67/pcsx2/soft-float-int/pcsx2/PS2Float.cpp`
+to confirm — the only differences are the include block, the `PS2FloatBits` block, the three
+`Common::` → `PS2FloatBits::` renames and the one `bit_cast` rename.
+
+### 2. `Config.h` — the soft-float fields are NOT bitfields in the union
+
+Upstream declares them inside the `bitset` union as `unsigned int vu0SoftFloat : 3;` etc.
+**That is broken under MSVC** and would have shipped silently: MSVC starts a fresh storage
+unit whenever a bitfield's declared *type* changes, so appending `unsigned int`-typed bits
+after the existing `bool`-typed ones grows the union from 4 to 8 bytes. Consequences:
+`bitset = 0` in `RecompilerOptions()` would leave the soft-float fields **uninitialised**, and
+`operator==` (which compares `bitset`) would not see them at all. Verified empirically on this
+toolchain (MSVC 14.51) with a standalone repro before changing the design.
+
+They are therefore three plain `u8` members outside the union, explicitly zeroed in the ctor
+and explicitly compared in `operator==`.
+
+### 3. `Pcsx2Config.cpp` — no `EntryFlagsBitfield`
+
+The PR adds `SettingsWrapper::EntryFlagsBitfield` to `common/` so the Qt UI can store one bool
+per flag. We store the mask itself with the existing `EntryBitfield` (keys `vu0SoftFloat` /
+`vu1SoftFloat` / `fpuSoftFloat` under `EmuCore/CPU/Recompiler`), masked with `SOFT_FLOAT_ALL`
+on load. `common/SettingsWrapper.*` is untouched. The PR's `pcsx2-qt` hunks are N/A.
+
+### 4. `pcsx2/VUops.cpp` — the whole VU half had to be re-expressed
+
+This is the substantive divergence. Upstream has already refactored the VU interpreter into
+`applyBinaryMACOp<Fn, MACOpDst>` / `applyTernaryMACOp<...>` templates plus a separate
+`pcsx2/VUflags.cpp`; the PR simply adds `applyAccurate*` template siblings and PS2Float
+overloads of `VU_MAC{x,y,z,w}_UPDATE`. **This fork predates that refactor**: `VUops.cpp` is
+4763 lines of longhand per-component code, there is no `VUflags.cpp`, no `MACOpDst`, no
+`_getDst<>`, and `VU_MAC{x,y,z,w}_UPDATE` are *macros* (`VUops.cpp:72-75`) forwarding to a
+single `VU_MAC_UPDATE(int, VURegs*, float)`.
+
+What was re-derived (all in one block inserted just above `vuADD_TriAceHack`):
+
+* **`VU_MAC_UPDATE(int, VURegs*, PS2Float)`** — a body-for-body port of the PR's
+  `VUflags.cpp` `VU_MAC_UPDATE(s32, VURegs*, PS2Float)`. Because the `VU_MAC*_UPDATE` macros
+  expand to a plain function call, adding this *overload* routes all ~300 existing call sites
+  automatically via ordinary overload resolution — nothing else had to change about them.
+* **`IsOverflowSet(VURegs*, int shift)`** — port of the PR's `VUflags.cpp` helper.
+* **`vuSoftIndex(VURegs*)`** — the PR writes `(VU == &VU1) ? 1 : 0`; this fork names the
+  register files `vuRegs[0]` / `vuRegs[1]`.
+* **`vuSoftBinaryMAC<Fn>` / `vuSoftTernaryMAC<Fn>` / `vuSoftAccTernaryMAC<Fn>`** — these
+  collapse the PR's six `applyAccurate*` templates into three. The PR needs separate
+  plain/Broadcast variants because its `Fn` reads `VU->VF[_Ft_]` itself; ours takes the four
+  "other operand" lanes as arguments, so the broadcast form is just "pass the same value four
+  times". `MACOpDst`/`_getDst<>` are replaced by an explicit `VECTOR* dst` argument (and
+  `vuSoftDstFd()` for the `_Fd_ == 0 → RDzero` rule this fork open-codes in every function).
+  The per-lane sequence (`_X`→`VU_MACx_UPDATE`/`VU_MACx_CLEAR`, … , then `VU_STAT_UPDATE`) is
+  identical to the PR's.
+* **`_vuAccurateOpADD/SUB/MUL/MADD/MSUB/MADDA/MSUBA`** — verbatim from the PR (including
+  `accfloat.of = oflw` before `MulAddAcc`/`MulSubAcc`). The PR's unused, and arguably buggy,
+  `vuAccurateMulAddAcc`/`vuAccurateMulSubAcc` free functions (`acc = oflw;` assigns a bool to
+  a `PS2Float`) were **not** ported — the PR does not call them either.
+
+**Every site where math was re-derived rather than copied** (all are "same operation, this
+fork's spelling"; the PS2Float call itself is always the PR's):
+
+1. **The 70 FMAC op functions** `_vu{ADD,SUB,MUL}{,i,q,x,y,z,w}`,
+   `_vu{ADD,SUB,MUL}A{,i,q,x,y,z,w}`, `_vu{MADD,MSUB}{,i,q,x,y,z,w}`,
+   `_vu{MADD,MSUB}A{,i,q,x,y,z,w}`. Upstream routes these through `vuADDbc`-style shared
+   helpers; this fork has 70 separate longhand bodies. Each got **one** guard line inserted as
+   the first statement, e.g.
+   `if (VU_SOFT_ADDSUB) { vuSoftBinaryMAC<_vuAccurateOpADD>(VU, vuSoftDstFd(VU), VU_SOFT_BC_Z); return; }`
+   The original body is left completely untouched below it. Guard conditions match the PR:
+   ADD/SUB → `CHECK_VU_SOFT_ADDSUB`, MUL → `CHECK_VU_SOFT_MUL`,
+   MADD/MSUB (incl. the A forms) → both.
+2. **`_vuADDi`** additionally has this fork's `CHECK_VUADDSUBHACK` (TriAce) branch. The soft
+   guard is placed *before* it, i.e. soft-float wins over the TriAce hack — same precedence
+   the PR ends up with (it replaces `vuADDbc_addsubhack` dispatch with the soft check first).
+3. **`_vuOPMULA` / `_vuOPMSUB`** — hand-written soft branches (3 lanes, no `_X.._W` mask, `.w`
+   untouched), mirroring the PR's versions exactly but using this fork's `RDzero` rule.
+4. **`_vuDIV` / `_vuSQRT` / `_vuRSQRT`** — hand-written soft branches. Structurally identical
+   to the PR's (`IsZero()`, `MIN/MAX_FLOATING_POINT_VALUE`, `ToDouble() < 0.0` for the sign
+   test, `statusflag` 0x10/0x20 bits, `Div`/`Sqrt`/`Rsqrt`). The one shape difference: this
+   fork's `_vuSQRT` has no zero special-case in the hard-float path either, matching upstream.
+5. **`_vuERCPR` / `_vuESQRT` / `_vuERSQRT`** — one-line soft branches calling
+   `PS2Float::ERCPR/ESQRT/ERSQRT`, same as the PR.
+
+**Not ported** from the PR's VUops hunks: the cosmetic whitespace churn in `_vuEATAN*`,
+`_vuESUM`, `_vuEEXP`, and the deletion of `vuADD_TriAceHack` (this fork still uses it in the
+non-soft `_vuADDi` path, and `CHECK_VUADDSUBHACK` is a live gamefix here).
+
+### 5. Recompiler routing — not in the PR at all
+
+PR #12001 only touches interpreters; on its own it does nothing while the recompilers are on.
+The routing is the `int_fallback_fpu_cop2` idea (#2990), implemented here as:
+
+* `FPURECOMPILE_CONSTCODE_SOFT(fn, xmminfo, softcheck)` in `x86/iR5900.h`, modelled on this
+  tree's existing `REC_FPUFUNC`.
+* `REC_COP2_mVU0_SOFT(f, opName, mode)` in `x86/microVU_Macro.inl`, modelled on this tree's
+  existing `INTERPRETATE_COP2_FUNC` (already used for `CALLMS`/`CALLMSR`, i.e. the
+  proven-correct way to hand a COP2 slot to the interpreter in this fork).
+
+Both checks are plain C++ `if`s evaluated at **block-compile time**, so with the option off
+the emitted code is byte-for-byte what it was before and there is zero emulation-time cost.
+
+Both use **narrow flush masks** (`FLUSH_SOFTFLOAT_COP1` / `FLUSH_SOFTFLOAT_COP2` in
+`x86/iCore.h`) rather than `FLUSH_INTERPRETER` (`0xfff`), and the COP2 one calls `xFastCall`
+directly instead of `recCall` (which would apply `0xfff` internally) and emits no per-op
+cycle store. Rationale and the two pre-flight checks are in the flush-cost section below.
+
