+## Measured cost, and the flush work that followed
+
+Benched on the level-4 driving arm, paraLLEl-GS, baseline = this branch with the option off
+(60.0 ticks/s, meanTick 6.92 ms):
+
+| arm | result | delta |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `pcsx2_softfloat = disabled` | 60.0 ticks/s, 6.92 ms | baseline |
+| `enabled`, scope `All` | 54.3 ticks/s, 12.22 ms | **+5.30 ms** |
+| `enabled`, scope `Add/Sub + Mul` | 59.9 flat, slowTicks 0 | **+1.44 ms** |
+
+The `Add/Sub + Mul` arm is the decisive one. It keeps **all 75 COP2 ops routed** through the
+interpreter and **keeps VU0 on `CpuIntVU0`**, and still costs only +1.44 ms. So:
+
+* **Routing/flush overhead + the VU0 interpreter together are ~1.44 ms** — *not* the
+  dominant cost. My initial hypothesis that per-op `FLUSH_INTERPRETER` would dominate was
+  wrong; recorded here so nobody re-derives it.
+* **The remaining ~3.9 ms is div/sqrt**: COP1 `DIV_S/SQRT_S/RSQRT_S` routing plus PS2Float's
+  `Div`/`Sqrt`/`Rsqrt`. That is unsurprising in hindsight — `PS2Float::Div` is an iterative
+  CSA radix divider (`DOI 10.1109/ARITH.1995.465363`), tens of iterations per call, versus
+  one `divss`.
+
+Even at +1.44 ms the results-screen replay path was heavy enough to trip the worker's 2%
+audio derivation (re-pinned 48000 → 46749), so trimming the fixed overhead is still worth
+having. Hence the flush work:
+
+**`FLUSH_SOFTFLOAT_COP1` = `FLUSH_FREE_XMM | FLUSH_PC | FLUSH_CODE`**
+**`FLUSH_SOFTFLOAT_COP2` = `FLUSH_FREE_XMM | FLUSH_FREE_VU0 | FLUSH_PC | FLUSH_CODE`**
+
+versus `FLUSH_INTERPRETER` (`0xfff`), which additionally does `FLUSH_ALL_X86 |
+FLUSH_FREE_TEMP_X86 | FLUSH_FREE_NONTEMP_X86 | FLUSH_CONSTANT_REGS` — i.e. it threw away the
+entire EE register allocation and constant propagation at *every* diverted opcode. Two
+pre-flight checks made this safe:
+
+1. **The call ABI is safe with any mask.** `iFlushCall` (`x86/ix86-32/iR5900-32.cpp:969-994`)
+   frees caller-saved GPRs *and* caller-saved XMMs unconditionally, outside every flag test.
+   The mask only controls what *additional* state is flushed.
+2. **No COP2 interpreter entry reads EE GPRs.** All 114 `cpuRegs.*` references in the `V*`
+   wrappers are `cpuRegs.code` (hence `FLUSH_CODE` is mandatory — it is *not* in
+   `FLUSH_FOR_POSSIBLE_MICRO_EXEC`, and the old code got it only via `recCall`'s `0xfff`).
+   The `_vu*` bodies reference `cpuRegs` zero times. Same for COP1: those 13 ops touch only
+   `fpuRegs`/`fpuRegs.fprc`.
+
+Freeing also routes through `mVUFreeCOP2XMMreg`/`mVUFreeCOP2GPR` (`iCore.cpp:719,731`,
+`iCore-32.cpp:441`), so microVU0's COP2 register cache is properly invalidated. And the COP2
+mask is a strict superset of `FLUSH_FOR_POSSIBLE_MICRO_EXEC`, which the `CALLMS` fallback
+already depends on before executing arbitrary VU0 code — so it is provably at least as safe
+as the path this fork already shipped.
+
+Also dropped from the COP2 path: `xADD(ptr64[&cpuRegs.cycle], scaleblockcycles_clear())`.
+That exists so `CALLMS` can sync VU timing before running a micro program; a plain FMAC needs
+no such sync, and it forced a mid-block cycle reconciliation plus a memory RMW per opcode.
+Cycles now accumulate to block end exactly as they do on the recompiled path. (`microVU.cpp`
+object shrank ~49 KB across the 75 macro expansions, which is the change taking effect.)
+
+**Not yet measured.** The flush change and the `vu0micro` split are hypothesis-driven; the
+next bench should A/B them.
+
