+## Live corruption incident (2026-08-08) — root cause was OPMSUB, not the flush
+
+Symptom on the deployed V1+V2 core: Stuntman physics exploding — cars and objects launched
+into space and twirling away after a snapshot load, respawns landing in random places.
+
+**Root cause: `_vuOPMSUB`'s soft branch clobbered its own source registers.** `VOPMSUB` is a
+cross product, so every lane reads components it does not write:
+
+```
+dst.x = ACC.x - Fs.y * Ft.z
+dst.y = ACC.y - Fs.z * Ft.x
+dst.z = ACC.z - Fs.x * Ft.y
+```
+
+Reading `VF[_Fs_]`/`VF[_Ft_]` inline while storing lane by lane means that with the ordinary
+in-place idiom (`_Fd_ == _Fs_` or `_Fd_ == _Ft_`) the store to `.x` destroys an operand the
+`.y`/`.z` lanes still need. Garbage cross products are garbage rotations, which is exactly
+the reported symptom. The hard-float path in the same function snapshots all six operands
+into locals first for precisely this reason, and so does the upstream PR — that snapshot was
+lost when the branch was collapsed. Fixed by restoring it.
+
+This bug was present in **every** build including V0, so it is unrelated to the flush work.
+
+**Audit of the same hazard class across the whole port** (do not re-derive this):
+
+* The three soft templates write lane N while reading only lane N of `VF[_Fs_]`/`ACC`. Same
+  component, and the RHS is fully evaluated before the assignment, so in-place `_Fd_ == _Fs_`
+  is safe.
+* Broadcast operands are passed as by-value function arguments, so they are evaluated at the
+  call site before any store — that is what makes every `.i/.q/.x/.y/.z/.w` form safe.
+* `OPMULA` is the only other cross-lane op, and it writes `ACC`, which can never alias a `VF`
+  register.
+* COP1 ops are scalar and compute into a `PS2Float` temp before storing.
+* **`OPMSUB` was the only aliasing hazard in the port.**
+
+### Re-audit of the V1 flush masks (they were reverted, but the reasoning stands)
+
+The narrow masks were suspected and reverted under time pressure. On re-reading, **the call
+ABI concern was unfounded**: `iFlushCall` (`x86/ix86-32/iR5900-32.cpp:969-994`) frees
+caller-saved GPRs and XMMs in an `if` that tests `xRegisterBase::IsCallerSaved(i)` /
+`xRegisterSSE::IsCallerSaved(i)` **first, outside every mask test** — the mask only selects
+what *additional* state is freed. And `IsCallerSaved` is correct for this target
+(`common/emitter/x86types.h:618` — under `_WIN32`, `id <= 2 || (id >= 8 && id <= 11)`, i.e.
+RAX/RCX/RDX/R8-R11, plus XMM0-5). So a narrower mask cannot leave a live value in a register
+the callee is allowed to clobber.
+
+What the narrow masks genuinely dropped was `FLUSH_CONSTANT_REGS`, `FLUSH_ALL_X86` and
+`FLUSH_FREE_{TEMP,NONTEMP}_X86` — i.e. EE GPRs stayed cached in callee-saved registers with
+memory possibly stale. That is only a hazard if the callee reads guest GPRs from memory, and
+the COP1/COP2 interpreter entries verifiably do not (every `cpuRegs.*` reference in the `V*`
+wrappers is `cpuRegs.code`).
+
+**Verdict: V1 is unconvicted, not exonerated** — it shipped in the same DLL as the OPMSUB bug,
+so the two could not be separated from the field report. **Do not retry it.** B1 already
+proved the entire routing + VU0-interpreter overhead is only +1.44 ms, so V1's *maximum*
+possible benefit is a fraction of that, against a nonzero risk in the hottest, least
+observable part of the recompiler. The ~3.9 ms of div/sqrt is where any remaining effort
+belongs.
+
+## Things to review / watch
+
+* **The COP2 fallback is deliberately all-or-nothing over the `mode & 0x10` set.**
+  `setupMacroOp`/`endMacroOp` implement a protocol where a *denormalized* status flag can live
+  in `vuRegs[0].VI[REG_STATUS_FLAG]` between two consecutive macro ops, driven by
+  `EEINST_COP2_{,DE}NORMALIZE_STATUS_FLAG` from `iR5900Analysis.cpp`. Diverting **every** op
+  that writes status/mac keeps this coherent (nobody writes a denormalized flag, nobody reads
+  one back; the interpreter's `SYNCMSFLAGS()` always leaves it normalized). Diverting only a
+  subset — e.g. only ADD/MUL but not MADD — would corrupt flags. If anyone later wants finer
+  granularity here, this is the trap.
+* **`CVT_S` and `C_cond_S` in `FPU.cpp` changed unconditionally** (the PR does the same). They
+  are inert in practice: those two opcodes are never routed to the interpreter, and the full
+  EE interpreter (`CHECK_EEREC == false`) is unreachable from libretro. Flagged in case a
+  future full-interpreter mode appears.
+* **`vu1SoftFloat` is wired but never set to non-zero.** All the VU-side code honours it, so
+  enabling VU1 soft-float later is a one-line change in `libretro/main.cpp` — but note VU1 is
+  the *recompiled* path used for graphics and would also need `EnableVU1` forced off.
+* **`pcsx2_softfloat_scope` cannot currently narrow the COP2 routed set**, and making it do so
+  is not a small change — DIV/SQRT/RSQRT are mode `0x112`, so they sit inside the `mode & 0x10`
+  flag-protocol set described below. Cherry-picking them would need `CHECK_VU_FLAGHACK`
+  (`EmuConfig.Speedhacks.vuFlagHack`) forced off first, so that `setupMacroOp` always
+  denormalizes from memory and `endMacroOp` always normalizes, making a mixed
+  interpreted/macro set coherent. That trade costs speed on the ops that stay in macro mode.
+* **If div/sqrt cost has to come down further**, the options in rough order of effort are:
+  (1) leave COP1 `DIV_S/SQRT_S/RSQRT_S` recompiled via `scope` and check whether Stuntman's
+  determinism survives; (2) the `vuFlagHack` route above, to keep COP2 div/sqrt in macro mode
+  too; (3) a soft-float *recompiled* path that emits calls to `PS2Float::*` with operands kept
+  in registers, removing the flush entirely and leaving only the software math.
+* **Perf is expected to be bad when enabled.** Every EE FPU arithmetic op and every COP2 FMAC
+  becomes a flushed call into C++, and VU0 micro programs lose the recompiler. Measure before
+  shipping this for Stuntman; if it is too slow, the `_scope` option is the first lever
+  (Stuntman's `dyStepRunge*` physics is add/sub + mul dominated).
+* **Not touched, deliberately:** `GameDatabaseBuiltin.cpp`, `GameIndex.yaml`, `iFPUd.cpp`
+  (the `CHECK_FPU_FULL` double path), any default setting.
+
+---
+
+## Build
+
+`build-core.bat` (CMake + Ninja + MSVC, `CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5`), build tree `bld/`.
+
+* Output: `D:\Arcade\build\lrps2\bld\libretro\pcsx2_libretro.dll`.
+  * V0 (`5ff4f9c`): 11,611,136 bytes.
+  * V1+V2 (`c98d183`): **11,606,528 bytes**. Note the SHA256 is **not** stable across
+    rebuilds of identical source — MSVC stamps a timestamp/PDB signature into the header, so
+    two builds of the same commit give the same length but different hashes. Verify a
+    deployment by length + the commit it was built from, not by hash alone.
+  * Pre-port deployed core was 11,317,760, so the port is **+2.6%**.
+* All 620 C++ TUs compile with `/O2 /Ob2 /DNDEBUG -std:c++17 -MT` (grepped out of
+  `bld/build.ninja`; also visible on the raw `cl.exe` line for `PS2Float.cpp`). The Dolphin
+  "Release without /O2" trap does not apply — no `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE` override was added.
+* `dumpbin /exports` shows the usual 25 entry points including `retro_run`, `retro_init`,
+  `retro_load_game`.
+* **Not deployed.** Nothing under `D:\ArcadeStorage` was touched and no worker/task was
+  recycled.
+
+## Suggested next arms
+
+Regression gate first: with `pcsx2_softfloat = disabled`, confirm a couple of non-Stuntman
+titles behave exactly as before. Then, off this one build:
+
+| arm | `softfloat` | `scope` | `vu0micro` | isolates |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| C1 | enabled | `All` | `interpreter` | V1 flush saving vs the +5.30 ms `All` arm |
+| C2 | enabled | `All` | `recompiler` | **(b)** exactly — cost of the VU0 micro interpreter |
+| C3 | enabled | `Add/Sub + Mul` | `recompiler` | cheapest arm that still has bit-exact COP2 macro |
+
+C3 is the current best candidate for holding 59.94. The open accuracy question it answers:
+whether Stuntman's chase determinism needs VU0 *micro* and div/sqrt bit-exactness at all, or
+only COP2 macro add/sub/mul.
